{"id":"72b83758-fd53-44d9-89c8-393e8b2abe02","kind":"note","ref":{"span":"d441c9c8b18bf2a1","key":"bablr1","file":"packages/editor/shared/architecture-zenfs.ts","baseline":{"blob":"5bc72ca78bdc772f4c2f7ef12ebabe584ff68f4d","start":0,"end":61},"shape":{"type":"ImportDeclaration","atoms":["import","type","{","ArchSink","}","from","\"","@brianjenkins94/observability","\""]},"context":{"after":"4e47137f5c19d132"}},"payload":{"text":"The workspace filesystem in the architecture reports. zen-fs is shared MEMORY, not a channel: every realm that\nmounts the workspace SharedArrayBuffer reads and writes the same bytes, with no message to observe. So each realm\nwraps its own `/workspace` store and reports the store operations it performs, as traffic from itself to the\n`zenfs` node — which is how the reports show who touches the workspace, how much, and (in the workbench) through\nwhich door: the vscode provider or a direct caller (isomorphic-git, the terminal's path walk).","wrote":["Brian Jenkins"]},"author":"local","createdAt":"2026-11-22T16:51:51.996Z","updatedAt":"2026-11-22T16:51:51.996Z"}
